\section{Diagnostic fragment matching}{

Fragment presence is evaluated using the peak m/z values in
\code{denoised_data$denoised_ms_data}, after denoising. Failed spectra
are excluded. The original MS2 peak arrays in \code{ms_data_raw} are
not used for fragment detection.

For an observed peak m/z value \code{observed} and a target fragment
m/z value \code{target}, a match satisfies:
\itemize{
\item Relative tolerance:
\code{abs((observed - target) / target) * 1e6 <= mass_error_tolerance_val}.
\item Absolute tolerance:
\code{abs(observed - target) <= mass_error_tolerance_val}.
}

A fragment is present if at least one peak matches its target.
Only fragments referenced in \code{diagnostic_frags_exp} are evaluated.
Including additional fragments in \code{diagnostic_frags_list} does
not require them to be present.

Each fragment name in the expression represents a logical presence
value. For example, \code{"HexNAc & HexNAc_ProA"} requires both fragments,
\code{"HexNAc | Hex"} requires either fragment, and
\code{"HexNAc & !Bisecting"} requires HexNAc without a detected
Bisecting fragment.

No additional intensity cutoff or fragment charge correction is
applied. Target values are used directly as m/z values.
Fragment tests are independent: the same observed peak can satisfy
multiple targets when their tolerance windows overlap.
Supply finite peak m/z values; missing values are not explicitly
removed before presence evaluation.
